{
  "abstract": "We study the eleven-vertex core $Q=K_6\\vee C_5$ in the search for a biplanar graph requiring ten colors. We prove that a graph of minimum order among all non-nine-colorable biplanar graphs cannot contain an induced $Q$ when every core vertex and every exterior hub neighbor has degree at most eleven. The exterior is otherwise arbitrary. The proof retains an exact extension relation: failure requires one common forbidden color at every core vertex. Counting components in the two actual planar layers then supplies private paths or two retained-root stars that enforce extension. If minimum order is restricted to maximum degree eleven, we instead identify a precise three-root residual; the unrestricted replacement can raise one root to degree twelve.\n\nA related abstract theorem holds for every $q\\geq8$: a graph partitioned into copies of $K_{q-3}\\vee C_5$ and one additional vertex, with maximum degree at most $q+2$, is $q$-colorable exactly when that vertex is not universal to a core. For $q=9$, we classify overlaps in $K_9$-free graphs of maximum degree eleven and prove nine-colorability for arbitrary core covers, including covers missing one vertex. Full covers also admit a coloring omitting a color on any ten-vertex boundary with spare degree. The proofs are independent of computation; compact constructive implementations and literal verifiers provide reproducibility checks. These results neither force core occurrence nor settle the Earth–Moon problem.",
